<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">New Delhi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">inaugurated <a href="/years/1931">1931</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, a planned city designed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edwin-lutyens">Edwin Lutyens</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herbert-baker">Herbert Baker</a> south of old Delhi. The move of the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> was announced at the Delhi Durbar of 1911, and the new city was inaugurated in 1931. After independence in 1947 it remained the seat of the government of the Republic of India.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/new-delhi.jpg" alt="New Delhi" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ronakshah1990 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Forecourt,_Rashtrapati_Bhavan_-_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> lies on the Indo-Gangetic Plain, on the floodplain of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamuna">Yamuna River</a>, and there is little difference in elevation across the city. The area was once part of the Aravali Range, of which the Delhi Ridge, sometimes called the Lungs of Delhi, is all that remains. The municipality covers 42.7 square kilometres and forms a small part of the larger metropolitan area of Delhi. In everyday speech Delhi and New Delhi are often used interchangeably, although they are distinct administrative entities.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Under British rule <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> was the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> until December 1911. It had become a centre of nationalist movements from the late nineteenth century, and Viceroy Curzon's partition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> produced a large upsurge in political activity, including assassinations of British officials, and a boycott of British goods. The colonial government reunited Bengal and moved the capital. Old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> had been the political centre of several empires, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> and, from 1649 to 1857, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>. The British government of India also judged that Delhi, in the centre of northern India, would be logistically easier to govern from.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-v">George V</a> laid the foundation stone of New Delhi, south of the old city, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-durbar">Delhi Durbar</a> of 1911. The land was acquired under the Land Acquisition Act of 1894. The city was designed by the British architects <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edwin-lutyens">Edwin Lutyens</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herbert-baker">Herbert Baker</a> and took about twenty years to build. Viceroy Irwin inaugurated it on 13 February 1931.</p><p>The plan was chosen above all to be a symbol of British power and supremacy. Its architecture follows a British Classical and Palladian tradition, with some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> and Islamic features that were included through the persistence of Viceroy Hardinge and historians such as E. B. Havell. The city is structured around two promenades, the Rajpath, or King's Way, which runs from the Rashtrapati Bhavan, formerly the Viceroy's House, on Raisina Hill to India Gate, and the Janpath, which crosses it at right angles.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and the seat of all three branches of its government, with the Rashtrapati Bhavan, Parliament House and the Supreme Court. It is part of the National Capital Territory of Delhi and is administered by the New Delhi Municipal Council. Hindi is the most widely spoken language and English is the main formal language of business and government. The city has about 145 foreign embassies and high commissions, and many regional offices of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> bodies. The National Museum, formally inaugurated on 15 August 1949, holds about 200,000 works of art covering over 5,000 years.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “New Delhi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Cultural life flourished among the new middle classes: Rabindranath Tagore won the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1913, and the architecture of <b>New Delhi</b>, designed by Edwin Lutyens and Herbert Baker and inaugurated in 1931, still houses the government of India.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-raj">British Raj · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>India hosted the G20 summit in <b>New Delhi</b> in September 2023.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-india">Republic of India · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1946 it sent missions to China and India, the first carrying a letter to Chiang Kai-shek that promised to maintain Tibet's independence, and in 1947 a delegation attended the Asian Relations Conference in <b>New Delhi</b>, perhaps the first public appearance of the Tibetan national flag.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tibet-1912-1951">Tibet (1912-1951) · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Bahá’í Houses of Worship, open to people of all faiths, have nine sides and a central dome, and their services consist of readings from the scriptures of all religions, without sermons; among the best known is the Lotus Temple in <b>New Delhi</b>, completed in 1986.</blockquote><a href="/religion/bahai-faith">Bahá'í Faith · Community &amp; Society</a></li><li><blockquote>The movement’s seventh summit was to meet in Baghdad in 1982, during the Iran–Iraq War; after Iranian aircraft bombed Baghdad’s Al-Dura refinery to show that its airspace was unsafe, the summit was held in <b>New Delhi</b> in March 1983.</blockquote><a href="/movement/non-aligned-movement">Non-Aligned Movement · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Miniature paintings of the Gita Govinda are held in museums from Bhubaneswar and <b>New Delhi</b> to New York, Zürich and Paris.</blockquote><a href="/songs/gita-govinda">Gita Govinda</a></li></ul></section>
